WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.182
This example combines population filtering with classification.

00:00:04.182 --> 00:00:11.677
The business question is about completed hospital stays, so we first remove admissions where discharge date is

00:00:11.677 --> 00:00:11.992
missing.

00:00:11.992 --> 00:00:20.162
An admission without a discharge date may still be ongoing, and it would not make sense to force

00:00:20.162 --> 00:00:22.132
it into a completed length-of-stay category.

00:00:22.132 --> 00:00:29.247
For the remaining rows, PostgreSQL can subtract the admission date from the discharge date to calculate the

00:00:29.247 --> 00:00:34.247
number of days. Then CASE classifies the result.

00:00:34.247 --> 00:00:36.752
Fewer than five days is Short.

00:00:36.752 --> 00:00:39.427
Five through ten days is Medium.

00:00:39.427 --> 00:00:41.057
Anything above ten days is Long.

00:00:41.057 --> 00:00:45.032
Notice the sequence. First define the population.

00:00:45.032 --> 00:00:46.772
Then calculate the measure. Then classify it.

00:00:46.772 --> 00:00:49.647
This pattern appears in many projects.

00:00:49.647 --> 00:00:55.342
A delivery company may filter to completed deliveries before calculating delivery time.

00:00:55.342 --> 00:01:01.082
A university may filter to completed courses before classifying grade outcomes.

00:01:01.082 --> 00:01:06.197
A support team may filter to closed tickets before measuring resolution time.

00:01:06.197 --> 00:01:09.192
Boundary testing matters here too.

00:01:09.192 --> 00:01:13.922
A stay of exactly five days should be Medium, not Short.

00:01:13.922 --> 00:01:17.352
A stay of exactly ten days is still Medium.

00:01:17.352 --> 00:01:24.697
Eleven becomes Long. When you build duration categories, write the boundaries in plain language first.

00:01:24.697 --> 00:01:29.027
Then make sure the CASE expression matches those words exactly.
